I wrote the critic review to `review-critic.json` in the Dominic Fike run folder. It passes the contract's review schema, and both input hashes match under the contract's own hashing. I didn't change any other file and didn't publish anything.

**Verdict:** the draft is honest about its weak spots, but the part that picks Type 7 over Type 6 doesn't hold up against the sources. There are 9 findings (8 major, 1 minor). I also raised the "contested typing" risk flag. The six-reviewer panel was already required because of the sensitive allegations.

The findings that matter most:
- **The main test contradicts the record.** The draft says that when a threat arrives, a Six braces and Fike leaves. But in his one documented physical threat (2016), he went toward what he thought was a gun to protect his brother. The danger-seeking Six, who charges at fear instead of bracing, is never addressed, though the site's Florence Pugh profile does test it.
- **"Brother, got you" is used out of context.** In the podcast, Levinson called the contract "just a formality" and raised Fike's drug problems first. "Brother, got you" was Fike promising to be reliable, not trusting a contract he never checked. The type FAQ repeats the misreading.
- **The strongest Six quotes are left out.** In the same Zane Lowe film he says stability is "always in another person," wants "an older mentor," and leans on a manager he tells to overrule him. The research notes had the first two, but the article counts his "codependent" line for Seven instead.
- **The opening scene leaves out facts.** Lowe's voiceover says the motel visit was Fike's own idea. The beach was the film's planned ending, and Lowe calls it his own habit. Who suggested the beach is unclear in the transcript.
- **Quotes read out of context.** One journalist's "redemption" line actually comes before Fike saying he'll "always be a drug addict," which cuts against the point the draft makes with it. A 2026 quote about using music "to not think about shit" is about the time before jail, but the draft puts it in the present tense.
- **Other gaps:** Type 9 is raised but never tested. Hunter Schafer's cheating account sits inside the type argument, and the "no public response" line leaves out her own statement that he has written about it in his songs.

I marked five passages to keep, including "He went looking for a story" and the house-arrest "Babydoll" ending.

I didn't check the beach line on video. One acceptance test requires that check before the beach idea is credited to Fike.
